I tested four sorting algorithms: Selection Sort, Insertion Sort,
Merge Sort, and Quick Sort.

I tested each algorithm using randomly generated lists of sizes 10,
100, 1,000, and 10,000. Each value in the list was a random integer
between 0 and 99,999.

For each list size, all four algorithms were tested on the same
randomly generated data. Each experiment was repeated multiple times
and the average runtime was calculated. Runtime was measured in
milliseconds using Kotlin's measureTime function.

Results

| List Size | Selection Sort | Insertion Sort | Merge Sort | Quick Sort |
|-----------|----------------|----------------|------------|------------|
| 10        | 0.106 ms       | 0.064 ms*      | 0.080 ms   | 0.083 ms   |
| 100       | 0.331 ms       | 0.182 ms       | 0.146 ms*  | 0.151 ms   |
| 1,000     | 1.987 ms       | 2.837 ms       | 0.390 ms*  | 0.784 ms   |
| 10,000    | 39.225 ms      | 22.428 ms      | 3.135 ms*  | 5.535 ms   |

Conclusions

For small lists, there was not a large difference between the four
sorting algorithms. Insertion Sort was actually the fastest for a
list of size 10.

As the list size increased, the difference became much more noticeable.
Merge Sort and Quick Sort performed much better than Selection Sort
and Insertion Sort on larger lists.

At a list size of 10,000, Merge Sort was the fastest algorithm at about
3.14 ms, followed by Quick Sort at about 5.53 ms. In comparison,
Insertion Sort took about 22.43 ms and Selection Sort took about
39.23 ms.

These results are consistent with the expected time complexities.
Selection Sort and Insertion Sort have O(n^2) average runtime, while
Merge Sort and Quick Sort have O(n log n) average runtime. Therefore,
the difference becomes much larger as the input size increases.

Overall, Merge Sort performed best on the larger random lists in this
experiment.